{"id":"63f5eb15-6540-4cbd-af4c-13d2cbbffb89","object":"chat.completion","created":1784995976,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    nodes_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    required = case['required_symbols']\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(nodes_dict.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = []\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = nodes_dict[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained = any(cid not in removed_set for cid in child_ids)\\n        if has_retained:\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol_counts[sym] - 1 < 0:\\n            continue\\n        ok = True\\n        for req in required:\\n            if req == sym and symbol_counts[req] - 1 == 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained = sorted([nid for nid in nodes_dict if nid not in removed_set])\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the task description. The case payload contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The goal: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" -> we need to remove removable nodes while ensuring that all required symbols remain represented, and we cannot remove root or nodes with retained children. Also consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes. So a traversal order: first deeper nodes (higher depth) then shallower, and for equal depth, tie-break by node ID (stable). The algorithm: iterate over nodes in that order, for each removable node, tentatively remove it if the set of symbols represented by retained nodes (after removal) still contains all required_symbols, and also the node has no retained children (i.e., all its children are also removed? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means if we remove a node, its children become orphaned? But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we should not attempt to remove a node that has any child that is retained. That implies we process nodes bottom-up: we remove leaf removable nodes first, then their parents if all children are removed? But the order is deeper first, which effectively does bottom-up. However, we must also ensure that after removal, required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. The remaining tree consists of nodes not removed. So we need to simulate removal decisions. The output: removed_node_ids (list of IDs in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs that remain after all removals). Also we must not mutate case, so we work on a copy of nodes or maintain sets.\n\nPlan: Parse case.nodes into a dict by id. Also build parent-child relationships. We'll have a set of removed_ids initially empty. We'll iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id (ascending? Use node ID as tie break, but stable; we can sort by (-depth, id) ascending id). For each node, if node.removable is True, and node.id is not root (root is id \"root\" usually, but we can check parent is null), and the node's children are all already removed? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means we should only consider removing a node if all its children are already removed (or it has no children). But we are processing deeper first, so children (which have higher depth) would be processed before parent. So when we get to a parent, its children would have been considered and possibly removed already. However, we need to check that all children of that node are in removed_ids set. If any child is still retained (not removed), then we cannot remove the parent. Then we simulate removal: temporarily assume we remove it, and check if all required_symbols are still present among the IDs not removed (including that node being removed). But the required_symbols are on nodes, not just any node? It says \"all required symbols remain represented\" meaning that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node with that symbol that is not removed. So we need to know the symbols of retained nodes after removal. So we can compute current symbols set of retained nodes (including nodes not removed yet). Then for a candidate removal, we check if after removing that node, the required symbols are still covered. If yes, we mark it as removed and add to removed_ids list in order. We also need to consider that removing a node might affect symbol coverage.\n\nImportant: The root might have symbol \"root\". It is not removable. Required_symbols likely include \"root\" and maybe others. So we must ensure that after removing nodes, root symbol is still there (it is, because root is not removable). Also we need to consider that nodes with same symbol could exist; removal of one may be okay if another node with same symbol remains.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build nodes_dict {id: node}\n- Build children list for each node (or a set of children ids)\n- Initialize removed_set = set()\n- Initialize retained_set = set of all node ids\n- We'll track removed_order list.\n\n- Sort node_ids by depth descending, then id lexicographically (stable). We'll iterate over that list.\n\n- For each node_id in sorted order:\n  - node = nodes_dict[node_id]\n  - if not node['removable']: continue\n  - if node['parent'] is None: continue (root)\n  - Check children: if node has children and any child id not in removed_set: then cannot remove (has retained children). So skip.\n  - else:\n    - Consider removal: temporarily compute new retained symbols: start from current retained symbols set, but remove node's symbol from count? Better: maintain a dictionary mapping symbol to count of retained nodes with that symbol. Initially build counts from all nodes. Then for each removal, decrement count for that node's symbol. If any required symbol count becomes 0 after decrement, removal invalid. If valid, then actually remove: add node_id to removed_set, append to removed_order, update counts.\n    - But note: we need to ensure that after removal, required symbols are still represented. So we need to track counts of symbols among retained nodes.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Build dict nodes.\n2. Build children mapping: initialize empty list for each node id, then for each node, if parent not None, add child id to parent's children list.\n3. Compute symbol_counts: dict symbol -> int, starting from all nodes.\n4. required_symbols = case['required_symbols'] (list).\n5. Sort node_ids: sorted(nodes_dict.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\n6. removed_order = []\n7. For each nid in sorted list:\n   node = nodes_dict[nid]\n   if not node['removable']: continue\n   if node['parent'] is None: continue\n   # check retained children\n   children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n   has_retained_child = any(cid not in removed_set for cid in children)\n   if has_retained_child: continue\n   # try removal\n   symbol = node['symbol']\n   # check if removing would violate required symbols\n   new_count = symbol_counts[symbol] - 1\n   if new_count < 0: continue # shouldn't happen\n   # check if any required symbol would become zero\n   ok = True\n   for req_sym in required_symbols:\n       if req_sym == symbol and new_count == 0:\n           ok = False\n           break\n   if ok:\n       # perform removal\n       removed_set.add(nid)\n       removed_order.append(nid)\n       symbol_counts[symbol] = new_count\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes_dict if nid not in removed_set])\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nNote: The requirement \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We did that by sorting by (-depth, id). That's stable.\n\nAlso \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We skip root, and we skip nodes with retained children. However, we also need to consider \"a required symbol\" meaning a node whose symbol is required? The phrase: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It might mean we should never remove a node that itself is a required symbol (i.e., its symbol is in required_symbols)? But then it says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies we can remove a node with a required symbol if another node with same symbol remains. For example, if there are two nodes with symbol \"s1\" and both are removable, we could remove one if the other remains. The prohibition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" likely means we cannot remove a node if its symbol is required and it's the only node with that symbol. But the algorithm already enforces that via the check. However, the phrase could be interpreted as \"never remove a node that is itself a required symbol\" regardless of duplicates. But in the given fixtures, \"root\" is required and not removable, so fine. Other required symbols like \"s1\" appear on multiple nodes. Let's check fixture: required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. root is not removable. s1 appears on n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable). So n4 is removable and its symbol is required. Should we allow removal of n4? The condition \"never remove a required symbol\" would forbid it. But the task says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests that if we can ensure representation, removal is allowed. I think the intent is to allow removal of a node with a required symbol as long as another node with that same symbol remains. The phrase \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" might be a summary: root is never removable, a required symbol (meaning a node that is the only representative? ambiguous). However, looking at Perses algorithm: it removes nodes that are not necessary to preserve interestingness (here, required symbols). Typically, it can remove nodes with required symbols if they are redundant. So I'll allow removal if required symbols still represented. If the required symbol is unique, it will be blocked. In fixtures, \"root\" is unique and not removable, so fine.\n\nThus our algorithm passes.\n\nWe should also consider: \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So no external data.\n\nEdge cases: If case has no removable nodes, removed_order empty, retained all.\n\nImplement solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nNow we need to produce the Python source as a JSON string inside {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. Must escape properly.\n\nWe'll write the implementation as a function.\n\nLet's write code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    nodes_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get('parent')\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    required = case['required_symbols']\n    # sort by depth descending, then id\n    sorted_ids = sorted(nodes_dict.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removed = []\n    removed_set = set()\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\n        node = nodes_dict[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            continue  # root\n        # check retained children\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        has_retained = any(cid not in removed_set for cid in child_ids)\n        if has_retained:\n            continue\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        # tentatively remove\n        if symbol_counts[sym] - 1 < 0:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols\n        ok = True\n        for req in required:\n            if req == sym and symbol_counts[req] - 1 == 0:\n                ok = False\n                break\n            # also check if any other required symbol becomes zero? No, only the one we remove affects its own count.\n            # We only need to check the symbol of the node we are removing.\n            # But if we remove a node that does not have a required symbol, it's fine.\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # perform removal\n        removed_set.add(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    retained = sorted([nid for nid in nodes_dict if nid not in removed_set])\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}\n\nWe need to ensure it returns JSON-serializable. The IDs are strings. The list of removed is in order.\n\nCheck with first fixture case: nodes with depths. Let's simulate quickly: root depth0, n1 depth1 removable false, n2 depth1 removable true, n3 depth1 removable false, n4 depth2 removable true child of n2, n5 depth2 child of n3 removable false, n6 depth2 child of n1 removable true. required_symbols: root, s1. symbols: root: root, n1 s1, n2 s2, n3 s0, n4 s1, n5 s2, n6 s0. Sorted order by depth descending: depth2: n4, n5, n6 (order by id: n4, n5, n6). depth1: n1,n2,n3 (n1,n2,n3). depth0: root. Process n4 (removable true, parent n2). Children none? n4 has no children. Check retained children: none. Symbol s1. symbol_counts[s1]=2 (n1 and n4). Removing n4: new count s1=1 which >0, so ok. Remove n4. removed_set={n4}, removed=[n4]. symbol_counts[s1]=1. Next n5: removable false skip. n6: removable true, parent n1, no children, symbol s0. symbol_counts[s0]=2 (n3 and n6). Removing n6: s0 count becomes 1 >0, ok. Remove n6. removed_set={n4,n6}, removed=[n4,n6]. symbol_counts[s0]=1. Next depth1: n1: removable false skip. n2: removable true, parent root. children: n4? n4 is child, but n4 is removed, so no retained children. Symbol s2. symbol_counts[s2]=2 (n2 and n5). Removing n2: s2 count becomes 1 >0, ok. Remove n2. removed_set now {n4,n6,n2}, removed=[n4,n6,n2]. symbol_counts[s2]=1. n3: removable false skip. root: not removable. Final retained: root, n1, n3, n5. IDs sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root']? Actually alphabetical? 'n1','n3','n5','root'. That seems plausible. But check if required symbols: root and s1 -> root present, s1 present via n1. Good.\n\nBut we need to verify that the node n2's children: n4 is removed, so retained children check passes. The algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow we need to encode the Python source as a JSON string. We'll write the function and then json.dumps it.\n\nWe must be careful with escaping. We'll produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the string.\n\nI'll write the final answer as a JSON object."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":3626,"total_tokens":5795,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3209},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}